WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.650
I am not inside a fusion reactor. It'd be
wonderful if I was,

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:07.749
the Joint European Torus reactor here at Culham
Centre for Fusion Energy

00:00:07.749 --> 00:00:10.630
is behind some blast doors just a few metres
away,

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.890
but that's not really somewhere humans are
meant to go.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.269
Not even for maintenance: it's not really
dangerous

00:00:16.269 --> 00:00:18.939
in the same way that current nuclear power
plants are,

00:00:18.939 --> 00:00:24.769
but it contains toxic beryllium and low-level
radioactive... stuff.

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:26.730
This is the mockup version of the reactor,

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.289
used for training. And by the way,

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:31.380
if the camerawork here seems a little bit
odd,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.230
that's because it's being done by... well,
here.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.309
Meet my camera operator.

00:00:36.309 --> 00:00:38.789
Say hi!

00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:41.510
I was going to say that my camera operator
is a robot,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.879
but that's not strictly true either. This
isn't a robot,

00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:47.019
it can't carry out anything automatically:

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:50.989
instead, it's a remote handling arm. Basically,

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:53.969
it's an extension of John over in the control
room.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:55.940
It's not as versatile as a human arm,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.589
but it's got enough degrees of freedom.

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:02.109
You are actually there, at that point,

00:01:02.109 --> 00:01:04.500
and not detached from it in a control room.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.020
You don't even realise you're doing it.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.490
You're actually thinking about the job as
you're there,

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.320
and you're imagining all the components around
you.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.340
Certainly from the views, we have a VR model
as well,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:19.700
which gives us good detail, so from that you
actually work at the coalface

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:23.509
imagining that the items are there right next
to you.

00:01:23.509 --> 00:01:28.149
It's got very good feedback. Even down to
something as thick as a layer of tape.

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:31.890
If you were to put that on a smooth surface
and run the grip across it

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.530
you'd be able to feel that level of detail.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.829
We have to service the ceiling as well,

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:38.619
so the transporter will drive the manipulator

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:40.750
upside down and up into position

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:46.439
so the operator stays in this position, but
gravity changes.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:50.460
So we have to teach the operators to deal
with that as well.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.659
There are all sorts of cool technologies for
remote presence

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:56.039
that could be used one day.

00:01:56.039 --> 00:01:59.729
Virtual reality. Gesture recognition. 3D cameras.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:04.109
All of which would be great: if they work
reliably, every time,

00:02:04.109 --> 00:02:06.820
and if they'll still work and be supported
after

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:12.010
10, 20 or 30 years of use in a radioactive
environment.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.390
This system can't be experimental, it can't
be risky,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:21.620
because the only way to fix or upgrade this
robot arm inside a reactor

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.569
is to send in a human.

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:26.260
You don't take risks for something like this:

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.269
you use something that is known to work safely.

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:33.690
And I did ask whether I could have a go controlling
the remote handling arm.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:38.209
For all those same reasons, the answer was
yes... but very, very slowly.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.060
